From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: mt6375-charger: take over charging

Follow the battery in software as the stock charger framework does: the
same temperature thresholds with their hysteresis, in whole degrees as
stock, 4.24 V above 45 C, and no charging at or above 50 C or below 0 C.
All values come from the stock tree of the Lenovo TB375FC. Charging
through the charge pumps stays out.

The charge voltage is at most 4.34 V, the lowest of the battery maintenance
table of that tree: stock uses it from 1050 cycles on or when the user asks
for it, and takes the lower of that and the JEITA voltage. Following the
table up by cycle count can come later. Stock also gives the fuel gauge a
voltage for what counts as full, which this leaves alone.

Nothing is written without a battery description and a fuel gauge in the
device tree. A battery description above the limits in the driver is not
clamped but refused, as it means a mistake.

Until the gauge has given a good temperature and voltage the chip goes on
as the bootloader set it, with two exceptions. A gauge that keeps making
no sense for three reads stops charging and nothing else: a temperature
or voltage out of range, or a temperature without a voltage. Taking that
as no reading would stop charging at 99 C and not at 101 C. And
inhibit-charge stops it at once, whatever the gauge does, and auto goes
back to what the chip did before. A gauge that cannot be read at all is
logged once and changes nothing, as it may just not be there yet. Only the
boost can delay taking over after a good reading, and what that reading
said still counts if the gauge then goes away.

After taking over, charging stops when the gauge fails three reads in a
row and comes back when the gauge does, where stock takes 27 C. It also
stops above the over-voltage threshold of the battery description, a check
that stock does not have. A voltage out of range neither sets nor clears
that, and it takes three low ones to clear it. VBUS is still followed while
the gauge fails. The gauge is read outside of the lock, so a slow one does
not hold up inhibit-charge or the boost.

Unlike stock, this keeps the safety timer of the chip running. Stock
turns it off at plug-in and counts 30 hours in software. Turning charging
off and on starts the timer anew, so a fault of the chip stops charging
until the source goes, as stock does with its timer: not suspend, nor
inhibit-charge, nor the temperature can bring it back. A fault found on
taking over counts too. Only a new driver instance forgets one. The limit
is ten hours in one go: without a fault, those same things start a new
count. It also leaves AICL and BC1.2 out: the latter would take D+ and D-
away from the USB controller. The input stays at the USB default of 500 mA
unless the USB port says more, and is back there with VBUS gone. The
charge current stays at 500 mA with charging off.

The charge and input currents go back to 500 mA when the driver stops
watching on removal and shutdown, and CHG_EN stays as it is. In suspend
charging stops once it is taken over, or if it should have already, where
stock wakes every 10 seconds to look, and when that fails the system stays
up with the work running. A kernel that hangs leaves them as they are, as
with stock.

There is no unbind through sysfs: a consumer of the boost like the TCPC
would go on to use freed memory, as the regulator core only warns about
it. rmmod is refused while one holds the regulator.

While the boost runs the driver stays away from its parameters and the
limits. Only inhibit-charge and suspend write to CHG_EN then: the boost can
end with the system asleep, and the work turns charging on again after it.
OTG_EN tells that the boost runs. The state of the chip lags behind it, so
the work also waits while that still shows the boost. The driver puts the
parameters back itself when starting or stopping the boost failed half way.
---
 drivers/power/supply/mt6375-charger.c | 1006 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 991 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/mt6375-charger.c b/drivers/power/supply/mt6375-charger.c
index 8b2aa0b..2fed29f 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/mt6375-charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/mt6375-charger.c
@@ -1,14 +1,23 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
 /*
- * MediaTek MT6375 charger: the boost that sources USB VBUS, and a read-only
- * view of battery charging
+ * MediaTek MT6375 charger: battery charging and the boost that sources USB VBUS
  *
- * Battery charging, the USB switch, BC1.2 and the interrupts stay as the
- * bootloader left them, and neither probing nor reading the state writes to the
- * chip. The first write comes when the regulator is enabled.
+ * The USB switch, BC1.2 and the charge pumps stay as the bootloader left them,
+ * and neither probing nor reading the state writes to the chip. The first
+ * write comes when the regulator is enabled, when charging is taken over or
+ * when it has to stop.
+ *
+ * Charging is only taken over with a description of the battery and a fuel
+ * gauge in the device tree, and once the gauge has given a good temperature and
+ * voltage. Until then the chip goes on as the bootloader set it, unless told to
+ * stop or the gauge keeps giving values that make no sense. After that the
+ * driver follows the temperature in software as stock does, and stops charging
+ * when it no longer knows the temperature.
  *
  * The registers, the parameters and their order come from the MediaTek driver
- * in the OnePlus MT6897 kernel.
+ * in the OnePlus MT6897 kernel, and the limits from the stock kernel of the
+ * Lenovo TB375FC. The chip's own safety timer, overvoltage and thermal
+ * protections are never turned off here. Its watchdog is, as on stock.
  *
  * Copyright (c) 2026 buntOS contributors
  */
@@ -19,11 +28,14 @@
 #include <linux/linear_range.h>
 #include <linux/mod_devicetable.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/pm.h>
 #include <linux/power_supply.h>
 #include <linux/property.h>
 #include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/regulator/driver.h>
+#include <linux/workqueue.h>
 
 #define MT6375_REG_TM_PAS_CODE1		0x107
 #define MT6375_REG_CHG_TOP1		0x120
@@ -73,9 +85,63 @@
 #define MT6375_OTG_UV			5000000
 #define MT6375_OTG_CC_1800MA		4
 
+/*
+ * The most this driver ever asks for, from the stock tree: 4.34 V
+ * (maintenance-v20-level0-cv, maintenance-v10-level3-cv), the lowest of its
+ * battery maintenance table, which a battery of any age can take, a charge
+ * current of 2 A (ichg, ac-charger-current) and an input of 2 A
+ * (ac-charger-input-current). Stock also goes to 4.5 V (battery-cv) with
+ * battery maintenance off, and to 3 A with a 3 A Type-C current, which stay out
+ * here. A battery description above these is refused, and so is a value above
+ * them from user space. The core driver has its own check of the same limits
+ * for every write.
+ */
+#define MT6375_CHG_CV_MAX_UV		4340000
+#define MT6375_CHG_CC_MAX_UA		2000000
+#define MT6375_CHG_AICR_MAX_UA		2000000
+/*
+ * usb-charger-current: for when nobody says more, and where the MediaTek LK2
+ * code leaves both
+ */
+#define MT6375_CHG_USB_UA		500000
+#define MT6375_CHG_CC_MIN_UA		300000
+#define MT6375_CHG_CV_MIN_UV		3900000
+#define MT6375_CHG_AICR_MIN_UA		100000
+/* maintenance-v10-level0-fg-cv: the most that stock ever tells the gauge */
+#define MT6375_CHG_OVP_MAX_UV		4530000
+/* jeita-temp-t3-to-t4-cv and temp-t3-thres, in whole degrees as on stock */
+#define MT6375_CHG_CV_WARM_UV		4240000
+#define MT6375_CHG_TEMP_WARM		45
+#define MT6375_CHG_TEMP_MIN		0
+#define MT6375_CHG_TEMP_MAX		50
+/*
+ * temp-t4-thres-minus-x-degree, temp-t3-thres-minus-x-degree and
+ * temp-t1-thres-plus-x-degree
+ */
+#define MT6375_CHG_TEMP_HYST		2
+/* The gauge reads below absolute zero or wildly hot when something is wrong */
+#define MT6375_CHG_TEMP_SANE_MIN	-40
+#define MT6375_CHG_TEMP_SANE_MAX	100
+/*
+ * A battery cannot read outside of these either. The power goes off below 2.5 V
+ * (uvlo-level), and the most this charger can reach is 4.934 V: the 4.71 V at
+ * the end of its CV range and 224 mV of IR compensation.
+ */
+#define MT6375_CHG_VBAT_SANE_MIN_UV	2500000
+#define MT6375_CHG_VBAT_SANE_MAX_UV	5000000
+/* vrec: how far under the voltage limit charging comes back */
+#define MT6375_CHG_VREC_UV		200000
+#define MT6375_CHG_VREC_200MV		1
+/* chg-tmr, chg-tmr-en: 5 hours from 0 in steps of 5 hours */
+#define MT6375_CHG_TMR_10H		1
+/* The stock charger thread looks every 10 seconds */
+#define MT6375_CHG_POLL_MS		10000
+#define MT6375_CHG_MAX_READ_FAILS	3
+
 enum mt6375_chg_field {
-	F_CHG_EN, F_BUCK_EN, F_HZ, F_IAICR, F_ILIM_EN, F_VMIVR, F_CV, F_CC,
-	F_CHG_TMR_EN, F_TE, F_IEOC, F_WDT_EN, F_IC_STAT, F_ST_PWR_RDY, F_MAX,
+	F_CHG_EN, F_BUCK_EN, F_HZ, F_IAICR, F_ILIM_EN, F_VMIVR, F_CV, F_VREC,
+	F_CC, F_CHG_TMR, F_CHG_TMR_EN, F_TE, F_IEOC, F_BLEED_DIS_EN, F_WDT_EN,
+	F_IC_STAT, F_ST_PWR_RDY, F_MAX,
 };
 
 static const struct reg_field mt6375_chg_reg_fields[F_MAX] = {
@@ -86,10 +152,13 @@ static const struct reg_field mt6375_chg_reg_fields[F_MAX] = {
 	[F_ILIM_EN] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_AICR, 7, 7),
 	[F_VMIVR] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_MIVR, 0, 6),
 	[F_CV] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_VCHG, 0, 6),
+	[F_VREC] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_VCHG, 7, 7),
 	[F_CC] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_ICHG, 0, 5),
+	[F_CHG_TMR] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_TMR, 4, 5),
 	[F_CHG_TMR_EN] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_TMR, 7, 7),
 	[F_TE] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_EOC, 1, 1),
 	[F_IEOC] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_EOC, 4, 7),
+	[F_BLEED_DIS_EN] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_VSYS, 7, 7),
 	[F_WDT_EN] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_WDT, 3, 3),
 	[F_IC_STAT] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_STAT, 0, 3),
 	[F_ST_PWR_RDY] = REG_FIELD(MT6375_REG_CHG_STAT0, 0, 0),
@@ -115,12 +184,58 @@ enum {
 	MT6375_CHG_STAT_FAULT,
 };
 
+enum mt6375_chg_temp {
+	MT6375_CHG_TEMP_UNKNOWN,
+	MT6375_CHG_TEMP_COLD,
+	MT6375_CHG_TEMP_NORMAL,
+	MT6375_CHG_TEMP_WARM_RANGE,
+	MT6375_CHG_TEMP_HOT,
+};
+
+/* What the work reads without holding the lock */
+struct mt6375_chg_reading {
+	int err;
+	int temp;
+	int vbat_uv;
+	int port_ua;
+};
+
 struct mt6375_chg {
 	struct device *dev;
 	struct regmap *regmap;
 	struct regmap_field *fields[F_MAX];
 	struct regulator_dev *rdev;
 	struct power_supply *psy;
+
+	/* Set at probe, then fixed */
+	bool wants_control;		/* the device tree asks for it */
+	bool can_control;		/* and the battery description is ok */
+	int cv_max_uv;
+	int cc_max_ua;
+	int ieoc_ua;
+	int ovp_uv;
+	int temp_cold;
+	int temp_hot;
+
+	struct delayed_work work;
+	struct mutex lock;		/* the fields below */
+	bool taken_over;
+	bool boost;			/* the boost parameters may be set */
+	bool overvoltage;
+	unsigned int ovp_clear;		/* low readings since then */
+	bool pwr_rdy;
+	bool fault;			/* no charging until the source goes */
+	enum mt6375_chg_temp temp;	/* the last one known, for hysteresis */
+	bool gauge_lost;
+	bool gauge_warned;
+	bool setup_failed;
+	unsigned int read_fails;
+	int behaviour;
+	bool held;			/* stopped, and not taken over yet */
+	unsigned int chg_en_before;	/* what goes back after that */
+	int user_cv_uv;
+	int user_cc_ua;
+	int user_aicr_ua;
 };
 
 /* The chip wants these set differently for the boost than for charging */
@@ -181,9 +296,8 @@ close:
 	return ret;
 }
 
-static int mt6375_otg_enable(struct regulator_dev *rdev)
+static int mt6375_otg_start(struct mt6375_chg *chg, struct regulator_dev *rdev)
 {
-	struct mt6375_chg *chg = rdev_get_drvdata(rdev);
 	unsigned int stat;
 	int ret;
 
@@ -220,31 +334,55 @@ static int mt6375_otg_enable(struct regulator_dev *rdev)
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	chg->boost = true;
 	ret = mt6375_chg_set_boost_params(chg, true);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
 	ret = regulator_enable_regmap(rdev);
 	if (ret) {
-		mt6375_chg_set_boost_params(chg, false);
+		if (!mt6375_chg_set_boost_params(chg, false))
+			chg->boost = false;
 		regulator_disable_regmap(rdev);
 	}
 
 	return ret;
 }
 
+static int mt6375_otg_enable(struct regulator_dev *rdev)
+{
+	struct mt6375_chg *chg = rdev_get_drvdata(rdev);
+	int ret;
+
+	mutex_lock(&chg->lock);
+	ret = mt6375_otg_start(chg, rdev);
+	mutex_unlock(&chg->lock);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
 static int mt6375_otg_disable(struct regulator_dev *rdev)
 {
 	struct mt6375_chg *chg = rdev_get_drvdata(rdev);
 	int ret;
 
+	mutex_lock(&chg->lock);
+	chg->boost = true;
 	ret = mt6375_chg_set_boost_params(chg, false);
 	if (ret)
-		return ret;
+		goto out;
 
+	/* An I2C error does not say the chip missed the write */
 	ret = regulator_disable_regmap(rdev);
-	if (ret)
+	if (ret && regulator_is_enabled_regmap(rdev) != 0) {
 		mt6375_chg_set_boost_params(chg, true);
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	ret = 0;
+	chg->boost = false;
+out:
+	mutex_unlock(&chg->lock);
 
 	return ret;
 }
@@ -411,6 +549,48 @@ static int mt6375_chg_get_charge_type(struct mt6375_chg *chg, int *val)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* What the driver knows. Called with the lock held */
+static int mt6375_chg_health(struct mt6375_chg *chg)
+{
+	if (chg->gauge_lost)
+		return POWER_SUPPLY_HEALTH_UNKNOWN;
+
+	switch (chg->temp) {
+	case MT6375_CHG_TEMP_COLD:
+		return POWER_SUPPLY_HEALTH_COLD;
+	case MT6375_CHG_TEMP_HOT:
+		return POWER_SUPPLY_HEALTH_OVERHEAT;
+	case MT6375_CHG_TEMP_UNKNOWN:
+		return POWER_SUPPLY_HEALTH_UNKNOWN;
+	default:
+		break;
+	}
+
+	if (chg->overvoltage)
+		return POWER_SUPPLY_HEALTH_OVERVOLTAGE;
+
+	return chg->fault ? POWER_SUPPLY_HEALTH_UNSPEC_FAILURE :
+			    POWER_SUPPLY_HEALTH_GOOD;
+}
+
+static int mt6375_chg_get_health(struct mt6375_chg *chg, int *val)
+{
+	unsigned int stat;
+	int ret;
+
+	mutex_lock(&chg->lock);
+	*val = mt6375_chg_health(chg);
+	mutex_unlock(&chg->lock);
+
+	ret = regmap_field_read(chg->fields[F_IC_STAT], &stat);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	if (stat == MT6375_CHG_STAT_FAULT)
+		*val = POWER_SUPPLY_HEALTH_UNSPEC_FAILURE;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int mt6375_chg_get_property(struct power_supply *psy,
 				   enum power_supply_property psp,
 				   union power_supply_propval *val)
@@ -426,6 +606,19 @@ static int mt6375_chg_get_property(struct power_supply *psy,
 		return mt6375_chg_get_status(chg, &val->intval);
 	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CHARGE_TYPE:
 		return mt6375_chg_get_charge_type(chg, &val->intval);
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_HEALTH:
+		return mt6375_chg_get_health(chg, &val->intval);
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CHARGE_BEHAVIOUR:
+		mutex_lock(&chg->lock);
+		val->intval = chg->behaviour;
+		mutex_unlock(&chg->lock);
+		return 0;
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_CURRENT_MAX:
+		val->intval = chg->cc_max_ua;
+		return 0;
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_VOLTAGE_MAX:
+		val->intval = chg->cv_max_uv;
+		return 0;
 	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_CURRENT:
 		return mt6375_chg_get_limit(chg, F_CC, &val->intval);
 	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_VOLTAGE:
@@ -441,12 +634,198 @@ static int mt6375_chg_get_property(struct power_supply *psy,
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Whether the chip sources VBUS, so that the work and the limits stay away: 1
+ * or 0, or an error. OTG_EN says it, before the state of the chip does and
+ * after. The flag goes up before the parameters of the boost are written and
+ * falls once they are back, as either can fail half way, so this puts them
+ * back. Called with the lock held.
+ */
+static int mt6375_chg_check_boost(struct mt6375_chg *chg)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = regulator_is_enabled_regmap(chg->rdev);
+	if (ret > 0 || (ret < 0 && chg->boost))
+		return 1;
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	if (chg->boost) {
+		ret = mt6375_chg_set_boost_params(chg, false);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+
+		chg->boost = false;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Turning charging off takes the fault of the chip away, and turning it on
+ * again would start a new safety timer. So a fault has to be seen before that,
+ * and it stays until the source goes.
+ */
+static void mt6375_chg_check_fault(struct mt6375_chg *chg)
+{
+	unsigned int stat, chg_en;
+
+	if (regmap_field_read(chg->fields[F_IC_STAT], &stat) ||
+	    stat != MT6375_CHG_STAT_FAULT ||
+	    regmap_field_read(chg->fields[F_CHG_EN], &chg_en) || !chg_en)
+		return;
+
+	if (!chg->fault)
+		dev_err(chg->dev, "Charger fault, stopping until the source goes\n");
+	chg->fault = true;
+}
+
+static int mt6375_chg_stop_charging(struct mt6375_chg *chg)
+{
+	mt6375_chg_check_fault(chg);
+
+	return regmap_field_write(chg->fields[F_CHG_EN], 0);
+}
+
+/* What stops charging even before it is taken over */
+static bool mt6375_chg_must_stop(struct mt6375_chg *chg)
+{
+	return chg->behaviour != POWER_SUPPLY_CHARGE_BEHAVIOUR_AUTO ||
+	       chg->read_fails >= MT6375_CHG_MAX_READ_FAILS ||
+	       chg->temp == MT6375_CHG_TEMP_HOT ||
+	       chg->temp == MT6375_CHG_TEMP_COLD ||
+	       chg->overvoltage;
+}
+
+/* Not taken over yet: back to what the chip did before inhibit-charge */
+static int mt6375_chg_release(struct mt6375_chg *chg)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!chg->held || chg->fault || mt6375_chg_must_stop(chg))
+		return 0;
+
+	ret = mt6375_chg_check_boost(chg);
+	if (ret)
+		return min(ret, 0);
+
+	ret = regmap_field_write(chg->fields[F_CHG_EN], chg->chg_en_before);
+	if (!ret)
+		chg->held = false;
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The way to stop charging in an emergency, so it does not wait for the work or
+ * the gauge. It writes now, also during the boost: charging is off then anyway,
+ * and this keeps it off for when the boost ends.
+ */
+static int mt6375_chg_set_behaviour(struct mt6375_chg *chg, int behaviour)
+{
+	switch (behaviour) {
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_CHARGE_BEHAVIOUR_INHIBIT_CHARGE:
+		if (!chg->taken_over && !chg->held) {
+			if (regmap_field_read(chg->fields[F_CHG_EN],
+					      &chg->chg_en_before))
+				chg->chg_en_before = 0;
+			chg->held = true;
+		}
+		chg->behaviour = behaviour;
+		return mt6375_chg_stop_charging(chg);
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_CHARGE_BEHAVIOUR_AUTO:
+		chg->behaviour = behaviour;
+		return mt6375_chg_release(chg);
+	default:
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * User space can only ask for less than the limits, or stop charging. Without a
+ * description of the battery the chip is not ours to change.
+ */
+static int mt6375_chg_set_property(struct power_supply *psy,
+				   enum power_supply_property psp,
+				   const union power_supply_propval *val)
+{
+	struct mt6375_chg *chg = power_supply_get_drvdata(psy);
+	int ret = 0;
+
+	mutex_lock(&chg->lock);
+	if (!chg->can_control) {
+		mutex_unlock(&chg->lock);
+		return -EPERM;
+	}
+
+	switch (psp) {
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CHARGE_BEHAVIOUR:
+		ret = mt6375_chg_set_behaviour(chg, val->intval);
+		break;
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_CURRENT:
+		if (val->intval < MT6375_CHG_CC_MIN_UA ||
+		    val->intval > chg->cc_max_ua)
+			ret = -EINVAL;
+		else
+			chg->user_cc_ua = val->intval;
+		break;
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CONSTANT_CHARGE_VOLTAGE:
+		if (val->intval < MT6375_CHG_CV_MIN_UV ||
+		    val->intval > chg->cv_max_uv)
+			ret = -EINVAL;
+		else
+			chg->user_cv_uv = val->intval;
+		break;
+	case POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_INPUT_CURRENT_LIMIT:
+		if (val->intval < MT6375_CHG_AICR_MIN_UA ||
+		    val->intval > MT6375_CHG_AICR_MAX_UA)
+			ret = -EINVAL;
+		else
+			chg->user_aicr_ua = val->intval;
+		break;
+	default:
+		ret = -EINVAL;
+		break;
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&chg->lock);
+
+	/* Also after a failed write: the work tries it again */
+	if (ret != -EINVAL)
+		mod_delayed_work(system_freezable_wq, &chg->work, 0);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static int mt6375_chg_property_is_writeable(struct power_supply *psy,
